{"id":"reverse_fly","name":"Reverse fly","cue":"Sweep your hands out wide without pinching your shoulder blades.","research":"research.json biomech.reverse_fly: seated facing the column, chest on the upright pad, trunk 5 deg forward, hips ~95 and knees 90, feet flat. Arms at shoulder height with soft (15 deg) fixed elbows, neutral grip on vertical handles; humerus plane ~91 (handles ~14 cm apart ~50 cm in front of the chest, scapulae protracted 12) -> a level T ~8 deg behind the frontal plane (humerus elevation ~90, plane ~-8 read back; scapulae retracted 5, never pinched), inside the research band (in line with or ~10 deg behind the shoulders). The ROM check limits horizontal abduction (plane) for a raised humerus instead of its ill-conditioned sagittal projection, which reads a level T behind the frontal plane as extension past -90. The handle hinge is the circle through the FK grip at A, mid-rep and B: a near-vertical axis just above each shoulder (tools/design/reverse_fly.mjs). Machine: a combination pec fly / rear delt type. The column, overhead frame and arms use the same hardware as pec_deck (tools/design/fly_unit.mjs), but the pad is a short chest pad on the column, sized for this seat position (a pec-deck-size back pad would reach the thighs and the upper arms), so the two are not one physical unit. The column stands just beyond the chest pad and stops at the beam of an overhead frame at 0.865 H, with a bearing housing on each hinge axis ~0.09 H above the head. Each moving arm is a lever with two rounded bends: a post down the hinge axis, a straight, nearly level run ~0.1 H above the upper arm, and a drop down the handle axis into the handle top, so from the rear camera it lies above the arm with sky between them and never crosses the rear delt.","targets":{"tol":10,"A":{"trunk_flexion":5,"hip_flexion":95,"knee_flexion":90,"ankle_dorsiflexion":0,"shoulder_elevation":90,"shoulder_plane":95,"elbow_flexion":15},"B":{"trunk_flexion":5,"shoulder_elevation":90,"s